1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,127
<font color="#000000">Foundation models have transformed language and
vision, yet tabular data,</font>

2
00:00:05,207 --> 00:00:08,158
<font color="#000000">the workhorse of data science, was left behind.</font>

3
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:10,415
<font color="#000000">Every table has a different schema,</font>

4
00:00:10,495 --> 00:00:13,897
<font color="#000000">so a model trained on one rarely transfers to another.</font>

5
00:00:13,977 --> 00:00:15,832
<font color="#000000">This paper introduces UniTabE,</font>

6
00:00:15,912 --> 00:00:20,088
<font color="#000000">a universal pretraining protocol that handles any
table uniformly.</font>

7
00:00:20,168 --> 00:00:23,699
<font color="#000000">It encodes each cell with a small module called TabUnit,</font>

8
00:00:23,779 --> 00:00:28,213
<font color="#000000">refines the table with a Transformer, and adapts
through text prompts.</font>

9
00:00:28,293 --> 00:00:32,340
<font color="#000000">Pretrained on thirteen billion Kaggle samples, it
beats XGBoost.</font>

10
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:37,994
<font color="#000000">Tabular data underpins applications like stock
prediction, real-estate forecasting,</font>

11
00:00:38,074 --> 00:00:39,219
<font color="#000000">and credit scoring.</font>

12
00:00:39,299 --> 00:00:43,735
<font color="#000000">Yet unlike text and images, it has no widely
adopted foundation model.</font>

13
00:00:43,815 --> 00:00:48,831
<font color="#000000">The reason: tables come in endless schemas,
different column names, data types,</font>

14
00:00:48,911 --> 00:00:53,088
<font color="#000000">and counts, so a model trained on one cannot be
reused on another.</font>

15
00:00:53,168 --> 00:00:57,926
<font color="#000000">Existing methods either flatten tables into text,
losing numerical meaning,</font>

16
00:00:58,006 --> 00:01:01,086
<font color="#000000">or assume train and test share a fixed structure.</font>

17
00:01:01,166 --> 00:01:03,860
<font color="#000000">Both block knowledge transfer across tasks.</font>

18
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:09,999
<font color="#000000">The promise of pretraining is that a model learns
general knowledge once from huge unlabeled data,</font>

19
00:01:10,079 --> 00:01:11,906
<font color="#000000">then transfers it to many tasks.</font>

20
00:01:11,986 --> 00:01:15,243
<font color="#000000">The authors ask whether this recipe can work for tables.</font>

21
00:01:15,323 --> 00:01:20,009
<font color="#000000">Three ingredients are needed: a way to represent
any table regardless of schema,</font>

22
00:01:20,089 --> 00:01:23,406
<font color="#000000">a training framework flexible enough for many objectives,</font>

23
00:01:23,486 --> 00:01:26,504
<font color="#000000">and a data source large enough to pretrain at scale.</font>

24
00:01:26,584 --> 00:01:30,556
<font color="#000000">Together, one tabular model could handle
classification, regression,</font>

25
00:01:30,636 --> 00:01:35,382
<font color="#000000">missing-value imputation, zero-shot prediction, and
tables that grow new columns,</font>

26
00:01:35,462 --> 00:01:37,110
<font color="#000000">without redesigning per task.</font>

27
00:01:37,490 --> 00:01:42,300
<font color="#000000">At UniTabE's heart is a module called TabUnit that
handles one cell at a time.</font>

28
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:46,563
<font color="#000000">It treats each cell as a key-value pair: the column
name is the key,</font>

29
00:01:46,643 --> 00:01:47,943
<font color="#000000">the content the value.</font>

30
00:01:48,023 --> 00:01:53,146
<font color="#000000">The name is embedded and mean-pooled, and a
data-type embedding, marking numerical,</font>

31
00:01:53,226 --> 00:01:56,407
<font color="#000000">categorical, or textual, is fused in through a gate,</font>

32
00:01:56,487 --> 00:02:00,795
<font color="#000000">so a salary column is handled whether its values
are numbers or words.</font>

33
00:02:00,875 --> 00:02:04,808
<font color="#000000">A linking layer injects the column vector into each
value token,</font>

34
00:02:04,888 --> 00:02:07,441
<font color="#000000">so attention links values to their column.</font>

35
00:02:07,521 --> 00:02:11,892
<font color="#000000">All cell vectors, plus a CLS token, pass through a
Transformer encoder.</font>

36
00:02:11,972 --> 00:02:14,149
<font color="#000000">A deliberately shallow LSTM decoder,</font>

37
00:02:14,229 --> 00:02:18,099
<font color="#000000">guided by a free-form prompt like fill in missing
value salary,</font>

38
00:02:18,179 --> 00:02:20,356
<font color="#000000">generates the answer token by token.</font>

39
00:02:20,436 --> 00:02:24,933
<font color="#000000">Keeping the decoder weak forces most knowledge into
the reusable encoder.</font>

40
00:02:25,013 --> 00:02:28,820
<font color="#000000">Pretraining mixes multi-cell masking and
contrastive learning.</font>

41
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:30,390
<font color="#000000">To pretrain at scale,</font>

42
00:02:30,470 --> 00:02:35,710
<font color="#000000">the team assembled a massive tabular dataset from
Kaggle: about seven terabytes spanning</font>

43
00:02:35,790 --> 00:02:37,645
<font color="#000000">three hundred and three domains,</font>

44
00:02:37,725 --> 00:02:42,482
<font color="#000000">two hundred eighty-three thousand tables, and
roughly thirteen billion examples.</font>

45
00:02:42,562 --> 00:02:47,621
<font color="#000000">On average each table has about twenty-nine
numerical columns and eight textual ones,</font>

46
00:02:47,701 --> 00:02:51,550
<font color="#000000">with investing, finance, and economics among the
largest domains.</font>

47
00:02:51,630 --> 00:02:54,573
<font color="#000000">For evaluation, they hold out twelve Kaggle tasks,</font>

48
00:02:54,653 --> 00:02:58,503
<font color="#000000">six classification and six regression, never seen
in pretraining,</font>

49
00:02:58,583 --> 00:03:03,340
<font color="#000000">plus seven widely used public benchmarks to compare
against established methods.</font>

50
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:06,321
<font color="#000000">The experiments show pretraining pays off.</font>

51
00:03:06,401 --> 00:03:08,618
<font color="#000000">On seven standard public benchmarks,</font>

52
00:03:08,698 --> 00:03:13,469
<font color="#000000">UniTabE reaches an average area-under-curve of
about zero point eight three,</font>

53
00:03:13,549 --> 00:03:17,618
<font color="#000000">beating Tapas, FT-Transformer, and the industry
favorite XGBoost.</font>

54
00:03:17,698 --> 00:03:22,469
<font color="#000000">On the twelve held-out Kaggle tasks, spanning
classification and regression,</font>

55
00:03:22,549 --> 00:03:26,234
<font color="#000000">it again outperforms XGBoost and a strong TransTab
variant.</font>

56
00:03:26,314 --> 00:03:28,787
<font color="#000000">It also performs well in zero-shot mode,</font>

57
00:03:28,867 --> 00:03:33,766
<font color="#000000">making accurate predictions on some datasets with
no task-specific finetuning,</font>

58
00:03:33,846 --> 00:03:37,340
<font color="#000000">evidence of genuine transferable reasoning about tables.</font>

59
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:40,454
<font color="#000000">Ablations confirm each part earns its place.</font>

60
00:03:40,534 --> 00:03:45,571
<font color="#000000">Removing the linking layer, which ties names to
values, causes the largest drop,</font>

61
00:03:45,651 --> 00:03:49,920
<font color="#000000">from an AUC of zero point eight three down to zero
point seven five.</font>

62
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,398
<font color="#000000">Removing the fuse layer that injects data-type
information also hurts,</font>

63
00:03:54,478 --> 00:03:56,125
<font color="#000000">and removing both is worse.</font>

64
00:03:56,205 --> 00:04:01,369
<font color="#000000">Dropping either pretraining objective, multi-cell
masking or contrastive learning,</font>

65
00:04:01,449 --> 00:04:02,904
<font color="#000000">reduces performance too.</font>

66
00:04:02,984 --> 00:04:06,742
<font color="#000000">Notably, a one-layer decoder beats three- or
six-layer ones,</font>

67
00:04:06,822 --> 00:04:09,940
<font color="#000000">supporting the choice to keep the decoder shallow.</font>

68
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:15,505
<font color="#000000">The pretraining paradigm that reshaped language and
vision can extend to tabular data.</font>

69
00:04:15,585 --> 00:04:22,301
<font color="#000000">The key is to respect table structure rather than
flatten it into text: represent each cell by its column name,</font>

70
00:04:22,381 --> 00:04:27,506
<font color="#000000">value, and data type, refine with a Transformer,
and adapt through free-form prompts.</font>

71
00:04:27,586 --> 00:04:28,914
<font color="#000000">Pretrained on billions,</font>

72
00:04:28,994 --> 00:04:33,077
<font color="#000000">UniTabE becomes a general tabular model that
transfers across tasks,</font>

73
00:04:33,157 --> 00:04:38,220
<font color="#000000">beats the XGBoost baseline, and handles missing
values and tables that gain columns.</font>
